    "result_text": "I've implemented `search_remote` in `kb/remote.py` and tested it against a real server; it works. `pytest -q` passes (2 tests).\n\n**How it works:** it creates an `httpx2.AsyncClient` with `Authorization: Bearer <token>` as a default header and passes it to `streamable_http_client(url, http_client=...)`. It then does the same thing as `search_local`: initialize the session, call `search` with `{\"query\": query}`, and return `split_titles(result)`. The client is closed when the function returns.\n\n**Things to know about mcp 2.2.0, the pinned version:**\n- It uses `httpx2`, not `httpx`, and `streamable_http_client` has no `headers=` argument. The docs say to pass your own client to set headers. `httpx2` is already installed as a dependency of `mcp`, so nothing was added to `pyproject.toml`. The code does now import it directly, though.\n- A client you pass in doesn't get the SDK's default timeouts, so I set the same ones by hand: 30s generally and 300s for reads, because the server may hold response streams open. The SDK's own helper for this is in a private module (`mcp.shared._httpx_utils`), so I didn't import it.\n\n**Test against a real server:** I ran a local MCP server over Streamable HTTP, with a wrapper that returns 401 for any request without the right header. The call returned `['Title A for vpn', 'Title B']`. All 6 HTTP requests carried `Bearer s3cret`: 4 POSTs, the GET stream and the closing DELETE.\n\n**One cleanup item:** I couldn't delete the test script. Please remove `_e2e_check.py` from the project root."
